'''''Dichorisandra thyrsiflora''''' or '''blue ginger''' is a [[species]] of tropical [[flowering plant]] which resembles [[ginger]] in growth and habit, but is actually related to the spiderworts (the genus ''[[Tradescantia]]''). The plant is native to the tropical [[woodland]]s of [[North America|North]], [[Central America|Central]] and [[South America]], especially in [[Atlantic Forest]] [[vegetation]] in [[Brazil]]. Of the family [[Commelinaceae]], it is cultivated for its handsome spotted stems and large shiny foliage which is held horizontally, surmounted by striking blue flowers.<ref>{{Cite web |title=How To Grow a Blue Ginger Plant {{!}} Neverland Blog |url=https://www.enterneverland.com/blog/how-to-grow-a-blue-ginger-plant |access-date=2022-07-01 |website=www.enterneverland.com |language=en}}</ref>

It was first described by the naturalist [[Johann Christian Mikan]] in 1823.<ref>{{APNI | name = Dichorisandra thyrsiflora | id = 48146}}</ref>

''D. thyrsiflora'' was first grown in England in 1822, and is recorded from Sir William MacArthur's catalogue in 1857 of plants he grew in Camden southwest of Sydney.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.hortuscamden.com/plants/view/dichorisandra_thyrsiflora_mikan/|title=Dichorisandra thyrsiflora Mikan|last=Colin Mills|date=12 March 2009|work=Hortus Camdenensis|accessdate=13 March 2011}}</ref> It has become naturalised in a small region of northeastern New South Wales in Australia.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://plantnet.rbgsyd.nsw.gov.au/cgi-bin/NSWfl.pl?page=nswfl&lvl=sp&name=Dichorisandra~thyrsiflora |title=''Dichorisandra thyrsiflora'' J.G.Mikan |accessdate=13 March 2011 |author=Peter G. Wilson |date=1993 |work=Plantnet - New South Wales Flora Online |publisher=Royal Botanic Gardens, Sydney}}</ref>

The Latin [[binomial nomenclature|specific epithet]] ''thyrsiflora'' means "with flower clusters resembling [[Thymus (plant)|thyme]]".<ref name=RHSLG>{{cite book |last=Harrison |first=Lorraine |title=RHS Latin for Gardeners |year=2012 |publisher=Mitchell Beazley |location=United Kingdom |isbn=184533731X }}</ref>

This plant has gained the [[Royal Horticultural Society]]'s [[Award of Garden Merit]].<ref name = RHSPF>{{cite web 
